<body>
  <main>
    <p class="wordmark"><a href="/">BROOKE REPORTS</a></p>

    <p class="eyebrow">Persuasion Spotter</p>
    <h1>Here's your <em>lesson.</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">Two files, on purpose. One goes to your teen. One stays with you, because it holds an activity that only works if they haven't seen it coming.</p>

    <div class="files">
      <section class="file">
        <p class="file-label">For your teen · 8 pages</p>
        <h2>Student packet</h2>
        <p>The idea, the six moves, the five pulls, three practice specimens, a week-long hunt, and the Spot the Pull worksheet.</p>
        <a class="btn secondary" href="/assets/downloads/persuasion-spotter-student.pdf" download>Download student packet</a>
      </section>

      <section class="file">
        <p class="file-label">For you · 5 pages</p>
        <h2>Teacher's pages</h2>
        <p>Setup notes and The Letter, a live demonstration that lets the room feel it work.</p>
        <p class="note">Keep this one. Don't send it to students.</p>
        <a class="btn secondary" href="/assets/downloads/persuasion-spotter-teacher.pdf" download>Download teacher's pages</a>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="start">
      <h3>Before you teach it</h3>
      <p>Read the teacher's pages first, then run one Spot the Pull sheet on your own feed. You'll teach it better, and you won't be teaching it from above it.</p>
      <p>Caught a good one, or tried The Letter? Tell me how it went: <a href="mailto:hello@brookereports.com">hello@brookereports.com</a></p>
    </section>

    <footer>Made by Brookie at Brooke Reports</footer>
